A fisherman named Kael kept the tower’s clockwork heart: a small glass sphere that held a single, fragile memory. One stormy evening, a child named Nia came seeking a lost promise—the pledge she never spoke but kept inside her chest: to mend what many had broken, even if it required silence to begin. Kael released the sphere, and the memory unfurled: laughter stitched to sorrow, work braided with rest, a community that forgave as quickly as it forgot. The bells chimed with a guiding rhythm: listen first, then choose the sound you will send into the sea of days.

From that night, the town learned to trade noise for listening. Memory became the currency, and every return of trust was a bell rung in the harbor of time, reminding all that keeping promises requires hearing them through.
